.TH DVBTX.TOML 5 2026-11-08 "dvbtx 0.3.0" "dvbtx manual"
.SH NAME
dvbtx.toml \- configuration file of the dvbtx DVB transmitter
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B /etc/dvbtx/dvbtx.toml
.br
.B /etc/dvbtx/dvbtx.toml.\fIprofile\fP
.br
.B /etc/dvbtx/\fIinstance\fP.toml
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B dvbtx
reads one TOML file that describes the whole transmitter: global settings in plain tables
.RB ( [network] ,
.BR [epg] ,
.BR [web] ,
.BR [monitoring] ,
.BR [alerts] ,
.BR [ingest] ,
.BR [engine] ,
.BR [power] ,
.BR [defaults] ),
then any number of radios
.RB ( [[radio]] ),
multiplexes
.RB ( [[carrier]] ,
each placed on a radio) and services
.RB ( [[carrier.service]] ,
each in the multiplex above it).
Nothing is fixed in advance: as many radios, carriers per radio and services per carrier as the
hardware and the capacity allow.
.PP
Every key is optional except where stated; a missing key takes the default shown here.
Unknown keys of the plain tables are ignored.
The file is checked without transmitting by
.BR "dvbtx check \-c" " FILE,"
which also prints the frequency plan (LO, sample rate, offsets, useful rate of each multiplex and
the services with their LCN and service_id).
.PP
The file used is the one given with
.BR \-c ;
without
.BR \-c ,
.I /etc/dvbtx/dvbtx.toml
when it exists and the command line does not itself define a service, a frequency or a channel.
The configuration is merged in this order, each step overriding the previous one: built-in
defaults, the file, the quick setup options of
.BR dvbtx (1)
.RB ( \-\-driver ,
.BR \-\-channel ,
.BR \-\-service ...),
the
.B \-s
overrides, then the run options
.RB ( \-\-work\-dir ,
.BR \-\-web ,
.BR \-\-epg\-dir ,
.BR \-\-analysis\-export ,
.BR \-\-stats\-export ,
.BR \-\-no\-tsduck ).
.SH VALUES
.TP
.B Frequencies and rates
are in Hz and bit/s.
TOML integers
.RB ( 482000000 ,
.BR 482_000_000 ),
floats
.RB ( 482e6 )
and strings with a k, M or G multiplier optionally followed by Hz, b or bps
.RB ( \(dq482MHz\(dq ,
.BR \(dq27.5M\(dq ,
.BR \(dq8MHz\(dq )
are accepted by the radio keys
.BR gain ,
.BR center ,
.BR rate ,
.BR bandwidth ,
.BR lo_offset
and the carrier keys
.BR frequency ,
.BR bandwidth ,
.BR symbol_rate .
.BR ts_rate ,
.B lnb_lo
too.
.TP
.B Bit rates of the encoder
.RB ( video_bitrate ,
.BR max_auto_bitrate ,
.BR max_bitrate )
are numbers in bit/s or strings such as
.B \(dq4M\(dq
or
.BR \(dq3500k\(dq .
.B audio_bitrate
is handed to ffmpeg as written
.RB ( \(dq128k\(dq ).
.TP
.B Identifiers
.RB ( sid ,
.BR onid ,
.BR network_id ,
.BR pid_base ...)
are TOML integers, hexadecimal ones included
.RB ( 0x20FA ).
.B sid
may also be a string such as
.BR \(dq0x0101\(dq .
.TP
.B Durations
are in the unit their name ends with:
.B _ms
milliseconds,
.B _s
seconds,
.B _us
microseconds.
.SH TOP-LEVEL KEYS
.TP
\fBwork_dir\fR (string; default "/run/dvbtx")
Runtime directory: the generated engine configuration
.I engine.ini
and the SI tables
.RI ( si/ ).
Overridden by
.BR \-\-work\-dir .
.SH [network]
Network-wide service information (NIT, SDT, TOT) and text encoding.
.TP
\fBname\fR (string; default "dvbtx")
network_name_descriptor of the NIT, shown by some TVs.
.TP
\fBnetwork_id\fR (integer; default 0x20FA)
network_id of the NIT.
.TP
\fBonid\fR (integer; default 0x20FA)
original_network_id of every multiplex (a carrier may override it).
Many TVs ignore the logical channel numbers of an ONID that does not belong to their country;
0x20FA is France (terrestrial: 0x2000 + ISO 3166 numeric code).
.TP
\fBprovider\fR (string; default "dvbtx")
Default service provider name (SDT) of the services.
.TP
\fBcountry\fR (string; default "FRA")
Country of the TOT local_time_offset_descriptor, ISO 3166 alpha\-3.
.TP
\fBtimezone\fR (string; default "Europe/Paris")
IANA time zone of the TOT, and of XMLTV times given without offset.
An unknown zone is a configuration error.
.TP
\fBcharset\fR (string; default "iso8859\-9")
Encoding of the DVB text fields (names, EPG):
.B iso8859\-9
(0x05 prefix, as the French DTT; accents shown by the TVs),
.BR iso8859\-1 " to " iso8859\-15
(those of EN 300 468 annex A),
.B iso6937
(no prefix),
.BR utf8 ;
aliases
.BR latin1 ,
.BR latin5 ,
.BR latin9 ,
.BR tnt .
Pure ASCII text is sent without prefix, except in
.B iso6937
and
.BR utf8 .
.TP
\fBlcn_pds\fR (integer; default 0x28)
private_data_specifier sent before the logical_channel_descriptor (0x28: EACEM, as the French
DTT); 0: none.
.TP
\fBfrequency_in_nit\fR (boolean; default true)
false: centre_frequency 0xFFFFFFFF in the terrestrial delivery descriptors (DVB\-T, DVB\-T2), as
the French DTT does.
.TP
\fBsdt_other\fR (boolean; default false)
Also send SDT other (the services of the other multiplexes) on each multiplex.
.TP
\fBnit_period_ms\fR (integer; default 2000)
Repetition period of the NIT.
.TP
\fBsdt_period_ms\fR (integer; default 1000)
Repetition period of the SDT.
.SH [epg]
Programme guide: EIT present/following and schedule.
The guide is read only when
.B dir
or
.B urls
is set.
.TP
\fBdir\fR (string; default "")
Folder whose XMLTV files
.RI ( *.xml ,
.IR *.xml.gz )
and TSDuck EIT XML files are loaded, and re\-read when they change (checked every 10 minutes, at once on
.BR "systemctl reload dvbtx" ).
XMLTV channels are matched to a service by its
.BR epg_id ,
else by name; TSDuck EIT by service_id.
The package uses
.IR /var/lib/dvbtx/epg .
.TP
\fBurls\fR (list of strings; default [])
XMLTV URLs fetched every
.BR url_refresh_s .
.TP
\fBurl_refresh_s\fR (integer; default 3600)
Period of the URL fetches.
.TP
\fBdays\fR (integer; default 7)
Depth of the EIT schedule.
.TP
\fBschedule\fR (boolean; default true)
Send the EIT schedule; present/following is always sent.
.TP
\fBpf_cycle_ms\fR (integer; default 1000)
Repetition period of EIT present/following.
.TP
\fBschedule_cycle_ms\fR (integer; default 10000)
Repetition period of EIT schedule.
.SH [web]
The monitoring and control page, and its API (see
.BR dvbtx\-web (7)).
.TP
\fBenabled\fR (boolean; default true)
Serve the page.
.B \-\-web none
disables it.
.TP
\fBlisten\fR (string; default "127.0.0.1:8080")
HOST:PORT of the page;
.B \(dq0.0.0.0:8080\(dq
for the whole LAN.
Also the address
.BR dvbtx\-channel (1)
talks to.
.TP
\fBtoken\fR (string; default "")
If set, every request that changes something needs
.BR "Authorization: Bearer" " TOKEN."
.TP
\fBallow_exec_sources\fR (boolean; default false)
Let the web API (and
.B dvbtx channel
talking to it) set
.BR exec: ,
.BR pipe: ,
.B stdin:
sources and the raw
.BR ffmpeg_input / ffmpeg_output
options; the file may always use them.
.TP
\fBpreview_max_live\fR (integer; default 4)
Live channel previews at once.
.TP
\fBpreview_thumbnails\fR (boolean; default true)
Show the channel pictures on the page by default.
.TP
\fBpreview_thumb_every_s\fR (integer; default 60, at least 2)
A new picture per channel this often, while a page shows them; the CPU taken is about
proportional to 1/this.
.SH [monitoring]
.TP
\fBtsduck\fR (boolean or "auto"; default "auto")
Run
.B tsp \-P analyze
on the copy of each multiplex: true, false, or
.B auto
(when
.B tsp
is found).
.TP
\fBinterval\fR (integer; default 5)
Seconds between TSDuck reports.
.TP
\fBexport\fR (list of strings; default [])
Send each TSDuck JSON report to these
.BI udp:// host : port
or
.BI tcp:// host : port
addresses (also
.BR \-\-analysis\-export ).
.TP
\fBstats_export\fR (list of strings; default [])
Send the engine statistics and the active alerts (JSON, every second) there (also
.BR \-\-stats\-export ).
.TP
\fBtsp\fR (string; default "tsp")
TSDuck
.B tsp
command.
.SH [alerts]
Alerts are logged, shown on the page, and optionally sent out.
.TP
\fBmargin_min_ms\fR (number; default 150)
Warn when the decoding margin of a service drops below this (critical below 0).
.TP
\fBoverload_percent\fR (number; default 98.0)
Warn when a multiplex is this full.
.TP
\fBlate_ms\fR (number; default 300)
Warn when packets of a service leave this late.
.TP
\fBhold_s\fR (number; default 4)
A condition must last this long to raise an alert (radio underruns are raised at once).
.TP
\fBrepeat_s\fR (number; default 300)
An active alert is notified again this often.
.TP
\fBwebhook\fR (string; default "")
POST every alert event (JSON) to this URL.
.TP
\fBcommand\fR (string; default "")
Run this shell command for every alert event, with
.BR DVBTX_ALERT_EVENT " (raise, repeat, clear),"
.BR DVBTX_ALERT_LEVEL ,
.B DVBTX_ALERT_KEY
and
.B DVBTX_ALERT_MESSAGE
in its environment.
.TP
\fBinput_down_s\fR (number; default 10)
Warning when a running source has sent nothing for this long (its process alive, its multiplex on
the air); 0: never.
.SH [ingest]
The processes that turn each source into an MPEG\-TS on a local UDP port (see
.BR dvbtx\-sources (7)).
.TP
\fBbase_port\fR (integer; default 20100)
Local UDP port of input
.IR n :
.BR base_port " + " \fIn\fP.
.TP
\fBmonitor_base_port\fR (integer; default 20900)
Each multiplex
.I n
is copied to
.BI udp://127.0.0.1: port
with port =
.BR monitor_base_port " + " \fIn\fP
(TSDuck, previews; watch it with VLC).
.TP
\fBffmpeg\fR, \fBffprobe\fR, \fBstreamlink\fR, \fBytdlp\fR (strings; defaults "ffmpeg", "ffprobe", "streamlink", "yt\-dlp")
Commands used for the sources.
.TP
\fBloglevel\fR (string; default "warning")
ffmpeg
.BR \-loglevel .
.TP
\fBrestart_min_s\fR, \fBrestart_max_s\fR (numbers; defaults 1, 30)
Back\-off of a source process that exits: restarted after
.BR restart_min_s ,
doubled at each failure up to
.BR restart_max_s ,
back to the minimum after 60 s of running.
.TP
\fBstall_restart_s\fR (number; default 20)
A running source process that delivers nothing for this long is restarted; 0: never.
.TP
\fBdiscontinuity_restart\fR (integer; default 10)
A source whose ffmpeg reports this many timestamp discontinuities within 30 s is started again
beside the running one and swapped in without a cut; 0: never.
.TP
\fBgpu\fR (string; default "auto")
GPUs for the transcodes:
.B auto
(also
.BR \(dq\(dq ,
.BR on ,
.BR true ,
.BR yes ):
every usable adapter;
.BR off ,
.BR none ,
.BR cpu ,
.BR no ,
.B false
(or the boolean false): the CPU only;
or a comma\-separated list of adapter ids as
.B dvbtx gpu
prints them
.RB ( \(dq/dev/dri/renderD128,cuda:0\(dq ).
.TP
\fBgpu_cache\fR (string; default "")
File of the learnt GPU results;
.B \(dq\(dq
means
.I /var/lib/dvbtx/gpu.json
when that folder is writable, else
.IR ~/.cache/dvbtx/gpu.json .
.SH [engine]
The C engine
.BR dvbtx\-engine (8).
.TP
\fBbinary\fR (string; default "")
Path of the engine.
Empty: the
.B DVBTX_ENGINE
environment variable, then
.I engine/build/dvbtx\-engine
of the source checkout, then
.IR /usr/lib/dvbtx/dvbtx\-engine .
.TP
\fBlog_level\fR (string; default "info")
.BR debug ,
.BR info ,
.BR warn ,
.BR error .
.TP
\fBstats_interval_ms\fR (integer; default 1000)
Period of the engine statistics.
.TP
\fBcpus\fR (string; default "auto")
Where the engine threads run:
.B auto
keeps them off the cores that take the USB interrupts (a real\-time thread there delays the USB
work: gaps on air);
.BR all ;
or a CPU list such as
.BR \(dq1\-5\(dq .
.TP
\fBcpu_latency_us\fR (integer; default 0)
Processor wake\-up latency allowed while transmitting, held through
.IR /dev/cpu_dma_latency :
deep C\-states delay the USB DMA and empty the radio's FIFO.
0 blocks them all; a value such as 100 lets the cores sleep in shallow states; \-1: no request.
.BR auto :
starts at 0, then after each
.B cpu_latency_step_s
without a new gap on any radio lets in one idle state more (from the machine's own exit
latencies), up to
.BR cpu_latency_max_us ;
a gap brings it back at once to the last value that held, and the next try waits twice as long.
The value held is in the engine statistics.
.TP
\fBcpu_latency_max_us\fR (integer; default 100)
With
.BR auto :
the highest latency tried.
.TP
\fBcpu_latency_step_s\fR (integer; default 30)
With
.BR auto :
seconds without a gap before the next step.
.SH [power]
Machine power profiles applied by
.B dvbtx power
(see
.BR dvbtx\-power (8)):
.B save
while nothing is on air,
.B transmit
while a dvbtx unit runs.
Read at each profile switch; never needs a restart of dvbtx.
.TP
\fBsave_epp\fR, \fBtransmit_epp\fR (strings; defaults "power", "balance_power")
Energy/performance preference of the processor:
.BR performance ,
.BR balance_performance ,
.BR balance_power ,
.BR power .
.TP
\fBsave_turbo\fR, \fBtransmit_turbo\fR (booleans; defaults false, true)
Turbo / boost allowed.
.TP
\fBsave_aspm\fR, \fBtransmit_aspm\fR (strings; defaults "powersupersave", "default")
PCIe ASPM policy:
.BR default ,
.BR performance ,
.BR powersave ,
.BR powersupersave .
.TP
\fBsave_command\fR, \fBtransmit_command\fR (strings; default "")
Shell command run after the profile is applied.
.SH [defaults]
Settings of every service.
Each key can also be set in a
.B [[carrier.service]]
table, where it overrides the default for that service only.
.SS Backup and buffering
.TP
\fBbackup\fR (string or false; default "")
Source played while the service's own sources deliver nothing (any source form, looped; one
process shared by the services with the same backup).
"" or false: none.
The packaged
.I /etc/dvbtx/dvbtx.toml
sets
.IR /var/lib/dvbtx/backup.ts ,
created by
.BR "dvbtx make\-backup" .
.TP
\fBbackup_encode\fR (boolean; default false)
Re\-encode the backup clip instead of copying it.
.TP
\fBbuffer_ms\fR ("auto" or integer 100\(en30000; default "auto")
Cache between the arrival of a source and its transmission.
.BR auto :
6000 for live sources delivered in segments
.RB ( twitch:// ,
.BR ytdlp: ,
.BR streamlink: ,
HTTP URLs containing
.BR m3u8 ,
YouTube and Twitch URLs), 1000 otherwise.
.TP
\fBring_pkts\fR ("auto" or integer; default "auto")
Packets held per input;
.B auto
is the smallest power of two from 65536 holding the buffer plus 4 s at 15 Mbit/s.
.TP
\fBswitch_timeout_ms\fR (integer; default 3000)
Silence of the source, once its buffer has played out, before the service switches to its backup
(on a key frame).
.TP
\fBreturn_ms\fR (integer; default 10000)
The source must be stable this long before the service switches back from the backup.
.SS Several sources
.TP
\fBfailover_s\fR (number; default 10)
With several sources: the next one when the one playing has delivered nothing for this long.
.TP
\fBsource_start_s\fR (number; default 30)
\&... or has not started within this time.
.TP
\fBpreferred_retry_s\fR (number; default 300)
Not on the first source: the preferred ones are tried again this often; 0: never.
.TP
\fBunstable_switches\fR (integer; default 3)
A source is also left when its service fell to the backup this many times...
.TP
\fBunstable_window_s\fR (number; default 600)
\&... within this time; 0 switches: never.
.SS Multiplexing
.TP
\fBmax_late_ms\fR (integer; default 600)
Packets later than this are dropped.
.TP
\fBearly_ms\fR (integer; default 100)
Packets may leave this much in advance, to smooth the peaks.
.TP
\fBpcr_max_ms\fR (integer; default 35)
A PCR is inserted when the source leaves a longer gap.
.TP
\fBpmt_period_ms\fR (integer; default 100)
Repetition period of the service's PMT.
.TP
\fBpriority\fR (integer 1\(en10; default 5)
When a multiplex is overloaded, higher priorities are served first; the lower ones get the delays,
then the losses.
.TP
\fBbitrate_weight\fR (number; default 1)
Share of a re\-encoded service with
.BR "video_bitrate = \(dqauto\(dq" :
what the copied services (counted 6 Mbit/s each) and the fixed rates leave is split by weight
(SD 1, HD 2, Full HD 3, 4K 6).
.TP
\fBmax_bitrate\fR (bit rate; default "")
Cap of that share;
.B \(dq\(dq
means
.BR encode.max_auto_bitrate .
What a capped service does not take goes to the others.
.SS Reading the source
.TP
\fBmux_delay\fR (number; default 0.7)
ffmpeg
.B \-muxdelay
in seconds: the decoding margin given to the receiver.
Also sizes the encoder's buffer.
.TP
\fBreadrate\fR (boolean or "auto"; default "auto")
Read the input at real time (ffmpeg
.BR \-re ).
.BR auto :
yes, except for the sources that arrive at real time by themselves
.RB ( stdin: ,
.BR pipe: ,
FIFOs, RTMP, RTSP, SRT, UDP, RTP, TCP, V4L2, screen).
.TP
\fBreadrate_catchup\fR (number; default 2)
ffmpeg
.B \-readrate_catchup
of the network inputs read with
.BR \-re ,
when ffmpeg has it.
.TP
\fBhls\fR (string; default "auto")
How HLS URLs are read:
.B auto
(through streamlink when it is installed and nothing ffmpeg\-specific is asked),
.BR ffmpeg ,
.BR streamlink .
.TP
\fBloop\fR (boolean; default true)
Loop files and folders.
.TP
\fBmap\fR (list of strings; default ["0:v:0?", "0:a?"])
ffmpeg
.B \-map
of the streams kept: the first video, every audio track.
.TP
\fBcopy_audio_codecs\fR (list; default ["aac", "mp2", "mp3", "ac3", "eac3", "aac_latm"])
Audio codecs copied as they are; any other is re\-encoded (video copied).
Only DVB codecs count: a TV without E\-AC\-3 is served by removing
.BR eac3 .
.TP
\fBcodec_check\fR (boolean; default true)
Before copying a file, folder, HTTP URL, M3U entry or yt\-dlp stream, probe it with ffprobe:
video other than H.264, HEVC, MPEG\-2/1 is encoded, audio outside
.B copy_audio_codecs
too.
Skipped when
.B encode
is given explicitly.
.TP
\fBytdlp_format\fR (string; default "bv*[vcodec^=avc1][height<=1080]+ba[acodec^=mp4a]/b[vcodec^=avc1]/bv*[height<=1080]+ba/b")
yt\-dlp
.B \-f
selection of
.B ytdlp:
sources.
.TP
\fBrtsp_transport\fR (string; default "tcp")
ffmpeg
.B \-rtsp_transport
of
.B rtsp://
sources.
.TP
\fBffmpeg_input\fR, \fBffmpeg_output\fR (lists of strings; default none)
Extra ffmpeg arguments before the input, and before the output.
.SS [defaults.encode]
Encoder settings, used when a service is encoded: a capture source
.RB ( v4l2: ,
.BR screen: ,
.BR lavfi: ),
.B encode = true
or an
.B encode
table, or a codec found by
.BR codec_check .
A service's
.B encode = { key = value, ... }
table is merged over these.
.TP
\fBvideo_codec\fR (string; default "libx264")
.BR libx264 ,
.B libx265
and
.B mpeg2video
are generic: a GPU encoder of that family is used when one works (see
.BR hwaccel ).
Other names
.RB ( h264_vaapi ,
.BR hevc_nvenc ...)
are used as given; the encoder must exist in ffmpeg.
.TP
\fBpreset\fR (string; default "veryfast")
Encoder preset (libx264, libx265).
An NVENC encoder takes its own presets
.RB ( p1 " to " p7 ,
.BR fast ,
.BR hq ...);
an x264 one such as the default
.B veryfast
gives it
.BR p4 .
.TP
\fBtune\fR (string; default "")
libx264/libx265
.BR \-tune .
.TP
\fBvideo_bitrate\fR (bit rate or "auto"; default "auto")
Constant video rate.
.BR auto :
the share of the multiplex left by the other services (see
.BR bitrate_weight ),
at least 500 kbit/s.
Fixed rates that add up to more than the multiplex carries are a configuration error.
.TP
\fBmax_auto_bitrate\fR (bit rate; default "8M")
Cap of the
.B auto
rate.
.TP
\fBgop_s\fR (number; default 1.0)
Key frame interval in seconds (with
.BR fps ,
25 when unset).
.TP
\fBaudio_codec\fR (string; default "aac")
.BR aac ,
.BR mp2 ,
.BR ac3 ,
.BR eac3 .
.TP
\fBaudio_bitrate\fR (string; default "128k")
Audio rate; stereo always.
.TP
\fBaudio_rate\fR (integer; default 48000)
Audio sample rate.
.TP
\fBpix_fmt\fR (string; default "yuv420p")
Pixel format of a CPU encode.
.TP
\fBsize\fR (string; default "")
.IB W x H
such as
.BR \(dq1280x720\(dq :
scaled, aspect kept with black bars.
.TP
\fBfps\fR (string or number; default "")
Output frame rate.
.TP
\fBdeinterlace\fR (boolean; default false)
Deinterlace (yadif, or the GPU's).
.TP
\fBhwaccel\fR (string; default "")
.B \(dq\(dq
or
.BR auto :
a GPU when one works for the codec (according to
.BR ingest.gpu );
.B cpu
(or
.BR off ,
.BR none ,
.BR no ,
.BR false ):
never;
.BR vaapi ,
.BR qsv ,
.BR nvenc :
only that kind.
.TP
\fBdevice\fR (string; default "/dev/dri/renderD128")
Render node of an explicit
.B *_vaapi
codec.
.SH [[radio]]
One table per software radio.
All the carriers of a radio are synthesised together at one sample rate; the LO and the rate are
computed unless forced.
.TP
\fBname\fR (string; default "radio\fIN\fP")
Name the carriers refer to.
.TP
\fBdriver\fR (string; default "hackrf")
.B hackrf
(HackRF One),
.B uhd
(USRP B200/B210...),
.B soapy
(any SoapySDR transmitter),
.B null
(no radio: samples consumed at the nominal rate, for tests),
.B edgeqam
(an edgeQAM board on the network: see
.BR "edgeQAM boards" ).
.TP
\fBserial\fR (string; default "")
The device the radio is bound to: the serial number printed by
.BR "dvbtx devices" ,
given to the driver as
.BI serial= S
in its arguments.
Without it the driver opens the first device it finds, so two HackRF or two USRP may swap after a
restart or a replug.
A HackRF is also found by the last digits of its serial.
Two radios of the same driver cannot have the same serial, and a different
.B serial=
in
.B args
is refused.
.B "dvbtx devices \-\-save"
.I RADIO
and the web page
.RB ( dvbtx\-web (7))
fill it with the serial of a free device.
.TP
\fBargs\fR (string; default "")
Device arguments.
hackrf:
.BI serial= S
(as the
.B serial
key),
.BI rt= N
(SCHED_FIFO priority of its USB thread, default 60, 0: none),
.BI m0_poll= S
(seconds between reads of its gap counter),
.BI cb_log_ms= MS
(log USB callbacks later than that).
uhd:
.BR serial=...,type=b200 ,
.BR master_clock_rate=... ,
.BR num_send_frames=... ,
.B send_frame_size=...
(a B2xx on USB 3 gets 16360-byte frames unless given: half the packets of UHD's default, about a
third less CPU in UHD at 61 MS/s; larger ones break the stream).
soapy:
.BR driver=lime .
null:
.BI file= PATH
(writes the I/Q samples) with
.BR format=cs8 " or " cs16
(default cf32),
.BI absent= PATH
(the radio is missing while that file exists).
.B dvbtx devices
prints the radios plugged in and their serial numbers.
.TP
\fBgain\fR (number; default 30)
TX gain in dB: hackrf TX VGA 0\(en47, uhd 0\(en89.75.
.TP
\fBamp\fR (boolean; default false)
HackRF +14 dB amplifier.
.TP
\fBppm\fR (number; default 0.0)
Error of the radio's crystal in parts per million: the sample rate and the LO asked from the radio
are both divided by 1 + ppm/10^6 (HackRF, UHD, SoapySDR alike).
.TP
\fBcenter\fR (Hz; default 0)
LO frequency; 0: automatic, see
.BR "COMPUTED VALUES" .
A forced LO must lie on the subcarrier grid of the DVB\-T carriers.
.TP
\fBrate\fR (samples/s; default 0)
Sample rate; 0: automatic.
With DVB\-T carriers, a forced rate must be a multiple of their subcarrier spacing.
.TP
\fBbandwidth\fR (Hz; default 0)
Analog filter; 0: automatic (hackrf: the narrowest baseband filter that passes the band edges).
.TP
\fBlo_offset\fR ("auto" or Hz; default "auto")
With a single carrier and an automatic LO, the LO is put this far below the carrier, to keep its
leakage out of the channel;
.BR auto :
1.05 times half the occupied bandwidth, or, when that needs more than the radio's maximum rate,
the largest offset that fits, down to 0 (LO in the middle of the channel, its leakage there;
.B dvbtx check
says so).
.B dvbtx check
also warns when the rate is near the radio's practical limit (hackrf above 19.7 MS/s: USB FIFO
gaps; uhd B2xx above 56 MS/s): a lower symbol rate is then safer.
.TP
\fBbackoff_db\fR (number; default 13.0)
RMS level below full scale (room for the OFDM peaks).
.TP
\fBbuffer_ms\fR (integer; default 50)
Radio sample buffer.
.TP
\fBclock\fR (string; default "internal")
Reference clock source of a
.B uhd
radio
.RB ( external ,
.BR gpsdo ...).
.SS edgeQAM boards (driver = \(dqedgeqam\(dq)
An edgeQAM board modulates DVB\-T in its FPGA (Fishball 7010 board, AD9363): up to 4 multiplexes on
adjacent 8 MHz channels, each fed with its transport stream over UDP.
dvbtx then multiplexes only: its carriers are not synthesised, each multiplex is sent as a CBR stream
(7 packets per datagram, null packets included, PSI/SI complete) to the board's port of that
multiplex.
.PP
The board modulates on its own clock and buffers 8192 packets per multiplex: it inserts a null packet
when a buffer runs dry and drops packets when it is full.
dvbtx reads the fill level of each buffer (GET /status, 4 times a second, one poll per board) and
steers its rate on it: a ramp of +2\~% at the start until its
.B buffer_ms
waits, then a trim of at most
\(+-50 ppm moving by 0.02 ppm/s at most (0.54 Hz/s of the 27 MHz PCR clock), the PCRs stamped on
that clock.
When the board stops answering, the rate stays where it was (critical alert).
.PP
Its carriers are DVB\-T 2k, 64\-QAM, 7/8, GI 1/32, 8 MHz, the parameters of the FPGA (31.668 Mbit/s
each): they need not be given, and other values are refused.
Multiplex
.I k
of the board is on channel
.IR F " + (" k "\-1) \(mu 8 MHz,"
.I F
the lowest carrier of the radio (or its
.BR center ).
Keys of such a radio:
.TP
\fBaddress\fR (string; default "auto")
Host name or IPv4 address of the board;
.BR auto :
found by mDNS (service
.BR _dvbmod._udp ,
.BR avahi\-browse ,
package avahi\-utils), its UDP ports, API port and number of multiplexes read from its announcement;
the last board found is kept when mDNS does not answer.
.B "dvbtx devices"
lists the boards announced.
.TP
\fBserial\fR (string; default "")
With several boards: the one to use, by the
.B id
of its announcement (its Ethernet MAC, unique and stable:
.BR 0060881fa308 ),
its host name or its service name, as
.B "dvbtx devices"
lists them.
Host names alone are not stable: two boards with the same one are told apart by avahi in the order
they boot (\fBplutoplus\fR, \fBplutoplus\-2\fR).
When several boards answer and none is named, dvbtx refuses and lists them, rather than picking one.
.TP
\fBtune\fR (boolean; default true)
dvbtx sets the board: the frequency of its multiplex 1 and the multiplexes without a carrier
disabled (GET /set, not saved on the board), again whenever the board restarts.
false: its frequency and multiplexes are left as the board has them (its carriers' frequencies must
then match them).
The emission level (attenuation) is never touched: it is the board's setting.
.TP
\fBcenter\fR (Hz; default 0)
Frequency of its multiplex 1; 0: the lowest of its carriers.
.TP
\fBbuffer_ms\fR (integer; default 100 for a board)
Stream kept waiting in the board's buffers (100 ms: 2105 packets per multiplex), room for the jitter of
the network: 200 ms or more over Wi\-Fi, up to 290 (3/4 of its 8192 packets).
Above three times that (at most half way to 8192), the rate is lowered by 2\~% until it is back.
.TP
\fBmultiplexes\fR (integer; default 4)
Multiplexes of the board.
.TP
\fBports\fR (list; default the board's, 1001...), \fBapi\fR (integer; default the board's, 8080)
UDP port of each multiplex and port of its HTTP API, when they are not the board's defaults.
.SH [[carrier]]
One table per multiplex.
