.SH COMPUTED VALUES
.TP
.B LO frequency
One carrier: the frequency minus
.BR lo_offset .
Several: the middle of the occupied span.
Then snapped to the DVB\-T subcarrier grid, or to the DVB\-T2 P1 grid, else to a kHz.
.TP
.B Sample rate
At least twice the farthest band edge from the LO plus 150 kHz, within the driver's range
(hackrf 8\(en20 MS/s, uhd 0.2\(en61.44 MS/s).
With DVB\-T: a whole FFT size (factors 2, 3, 5, 7) times the subcarrier spacing.
Otherwise a rate of which every symbol rate is a simple fraction (exact frequency\-domain
synthesis), a whole multiple of the DVB\-T2 elementary rate when the radio carries T2.
Too many carriers for one radio is an error: split them over several radios.
.TP
.B Services
.BR sid ,
.BR lcn ,
.B pid_base
and
.B type
as above;
.B tsid
from the carrier position.
Changes made from
.BR dvbtx\-channel (1)
or the web page write
.BR sid ,
.B lcn
and
.B pid_base
explicitly into the services that relied on their position, so that nothing shifts.
.SH COMMAND-LINE OVERRIDES
.B \-s
.IR KEY = VALUE
(or
.BR \-\-set ,
repeatable) sets any key.
.I KEY
is a dotted path; a number selects an element of an array of tables (from 0), elements missing
are created:
.PP
.RS
.EX
\-s radio.0.gain=40
\-s carrier.1.frequency=498MHz
\-s carrier.0.service.1.source=/srv/videos/film.ts
\-s defaults.encode.video_bitrate=4M
\-s web.listen=0.0.0.0:8080
\-s \(aqcarrier.0.service.0.source=["http://a/1.m3u8", "http://a/2.m3u8"]\(aq
.EE
.RE
.PP
.I VALUE
is a number with a k/M/G multiplier
.RB ( 498MHz
becomes 498000000.0), else a TOML value (number, boolean, array, quoted string), else a plain
string.
The DVB\-T
.B mode
and DVB\-T2
.B fft
are names: read as numbers
.RB ( "\-s carrier.0.mode=8k"
gives 8000), they are turned back into
.BR 8k .
When
.B \-s
sets a
.B carrier.N.service
key, channel changes are not written back to the file.
.SH PROFILES
A profile is a copy of the file beside it, named
.IB FILE . NAME
.RI ( /etc/dvbtx/dvbtx.toml.night ),
.I NAME
being 1 to 40 letters, digits,
.B \-
or
.B _
.RB ( bak
and
.B tmp
excepted).
The web page saves the file as a profile, loads one over the file (validated first, the previous
file kept as
.IB FILE .bak\fR),
or deletes one.
Only the file itself is ever applied: a loaded profile waits for
.B Apply
like any other pending change.
.SH RELOAD
.B systemctl reload dvbtx
(SIGHUP) re\-reads the EPG folder, rebuilds the SI tables, and re\-reads the file:
.IP \(bu 2
the services are compared with those running and only the changed ones are touched: a new
source starts beside the old one and is swapped in without a cut; a change of name, number,
provider, type or EPG id only updates the SI tables; a change of
.BR sid ,
.BR pid_base ,
.BR switch_timeout_ms ,
.BR return_ms ,
.BR max_late_ms ,
.BR early_ms ,
.BR pcr_max_ms ,
.B pmt_period_ms
or
.B priority
re\-creates the service (a short cut);
.B [defaults]
changes apply this way to every service they affect;
.IP \(bu 2
a change in a
.B [[radio]]
or in a
.B [[carrier]]
table (its own keys,
.B enabled
included), or radios or multiplexes added or removed: nothing is applied, an error is logged;
restart dvbtx;
.IP \(bu 2
other changes (the plain tables) are logged as needing a restart, the channels being applied.
.PP
Reload works only when the configuration came from a file and the services were not set on the
command line.
.PP
.B Apply
on the web page goes further: when radios or multiplexes changed, the engine is restarted on the
new plan (a cut of a second or two on air), the sources that did not change keep running.
